### EVPN Subnet Slicing
* **Presenter:** Ali Sajassi
* **Slide Deck:** [draft-sajassi-bess-evpn-subnet-slicing](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-bess-draft-sajassi-bess-evpn-subnet-slicing-00)
* **Draft:** `draft-sajassi-bess-evpn-subnet-slicing`
* **Discussion:**
  * Tailored for enterprise/campus networks where subnets stretch widely across low-capacity access switches. Optimizes the routing component of IRB by partitioning subnets into slices handled by anchor PEs (distribution/border switches).
  * Access switches (non-anchor PEs) store only local host routes, forwarding non-local traffic to anchor PEs responsible for specific slices. Slices are advertised using Route Type 5, and leaves direct Route Type 2 advertisements to responsible anchors via targeted Route Targets.
  * In chat, Jie Dong asked if the term "slice" relates to IETF Network Slices (RFC 9543) defined in TEAS. Stephane Litkowski and Ali Sajassi clarified that "slicing" here refers to subnet partitioning and is distinct from TEAS network slicing.
  * Jorge Rabadan and others raised questions regarding anchor PE placement (border gateways vs. spines) and high availability/redundancy when anchor nodes fail. Ali clarified that anchor PEs are typically deployed in redundant pairs per slice.

